1
00:00:08,300 --> 00:00:11,936
In 2024, a team of geneticists did something

2
00:00:11,936 --> 00:00:13,837
no one had done before.

3
00:00:14,387 --> 00:00:17,731
They pulled DNA from the bones hidden inside

4
00:00:17,731 --> 00:00:20,466
the famous plaster casts of Pompeii.

5
00:00:21,016 --> 00:00:24,199
For a hundred and fifty years, those casts had

6
00:00:24,199 --> 00:00:25,306
told us stories.

7
00:00:25,856 --> 00:00:27,885
A mother, holding her child.

8
00:00:28,435 --> 00:00:31,138
Two sisters, in one last embrace.

9
00:00:31,687 --> 00:00:34,588
The DNA said those stories were wrong.

10
00:00:35,137 --> 00:00:38,287
The adult holding the child was a man, and he

11
00:00:38,287 --> 00:00:40,807
was not related to the child at all.

12
00:00:41,357 --> 00:00:44,728
So what really happened on the last day of Pompeii?

13
00:00:45,279 --> 00:00:47,683
Not the legend, but the evidence.

14
00:00:52,734 --> 00:00:55,042
This is Ancient World Files.

15
00:01:00,591 --> 00:01:03,981
In the first century A.D., Pompeii was a busy

16
00:01:03,981 --> 00:01:07,295
Roman town on the Bay of Naples, in southern

17
00:01:07,295 --> 00:01:07,746
Italy.

18
00:01:08,296 --> 00:01:10,914
About eleven thousand people lived there.

19
00:01:11,463 --> 00:01:15,522
It had a forum, temples, two theaters, an amphitheater,

20
00:01:15,522 --> 00:01:18,695
public baths, and dozens of bakeries, bars,

21
00:01:18,695 --> 00:01:19,728
and workshops.

22
00:01:20,278 --> 00:01:23,509
Its walls were covered with writing: election

23
00:01:23,509 --> 00:01:26,667
posters, advertisements, jokes, insults, and

24
00:01:26,667 --> 00:01:27,456
love notes.

25
00:01:28,007 --> 00:01:31,602
It was an ordinary place, full of ordinary people.

26
00:01:32,153 --> 00:01:35,501
One visitor scratched a complaint that survives

27
00:01:35,501 --> 00:01:37,924
in several places around the city.

28
00:01:38,474 --> 00:01:41,641
It reads: I am amazed, O wall, that you have

29
00:01:41,641 --> 00:01:44,808
not collapsed, carrying the tired scrawls of

30
00:01:44,808 --> 00:01:45,959
so many writers.

31
00:01:46,510 --> 00:01:49,749
In one bakery, archaeologists found eighty one

32
00:01:49,749 --> 00:01:52,917
loaves of bread still inside the oven, turned

33
00:01:52,917 --> 00:01:53,621
to carbon.

34
00:01:54,171 --> 00:01:57,509
And above the town rose a mountain covered in

35
00:01:57,509 --> 00:01:58,251
vineyards.

36
00:01:58,801 --> 00:02:01,037
The Romans called it Vesuvius.

37
00:02:01,587 --> 00:02:04,543
Almost no one thought of it as dangerous.

38
00:02:05,093 --> 00:02:08,052
The Greek geographer Strabo had noticed that

39
00:02:08,052 --> 00:02:11,080
the rocks near its summit looked scorched, as

40
00:02:11,080 --> 00:02:13,502
if they had once been eaten by fire.

41
00:02:14,051 --> 00:02:17,098
But the slopes were green, the soil was rich,

42
00:02:17,098 --> 00:02:19,129
and the local wine was famous.

43
00:02:22,680 --> 00:02:24,317
There had been a warning.

44
00:02:24,866 --> 00:02:27,989
Seventeen years earlier, a powerful earthquake

45
00:02:27,989 --> 00:02:29,211
struck the region.

46
00:02:29,762 --> 00:02:32,354
Temples fell and houses cracked.

47
00:02:32,905 --> 00:02:35,970
The philosopher Seneca wrote that a flock of

48
00:02:35,970 --> 00:02:38,478
six hundred sheep died near Pompeii.

49
00:02:39,027 --> 00:02:41,950
When the end came, parts of the city were still

50
00:02:41,950 --> 00:02:42,820
being rebuilt.

51
00:02:43,371 --> 00:02:46,329
In one house, painters had left a wall half

52
00:02:46,329 --> 00:02:46,949
finished.

53
00:02:47,498 --> 00:02:50,710
And in the days before the eruption, small tremors

54
00:02:50,710 --> 00:02:52,058
shook the area again.

55
00:02:52,609 --> 00:02:55,816
But tremors were common in Campania, and no

56
00:02:55,816 --> 00:02:57,009
one was alarmed.

57
00:03:05,560 --> 00:03:09,085
We know what happened next because of one eyewitness.

58
00:03:09,634 --> 00:03:13,119
His name was Pliny the Younger, and he was seventeen

59
00:03:13,119 --> 00:03:13,789
years old.

60
00:03:14,340 --> 00:03:17,264
He was staying with his uncle at Misenum, at

61
00:03:17,264 --> 00:03:20,719
the northern tip of the bay, about thirty kilometers

62
00:03:20,719 --> 00:03:21,915
from the mountain.

63
00:03:22,466 --> 00:03:25,954
His uncle was Pliny the Elder, a famous scholar

64
00:03:25,954 --> 00:03:29,146
who had written an enormous encyclopedia of

65
00:03:29,146 --> 00:03:30,482
the natural world.

66
00:03:31,032 --> 00:03:34,033
He was also the commander of the Roman fleet

67
00:03:34,033 --> 00:03:35,123
stationed there.

68
00:03:35,674 --> 00:03:38,823
Around one o'clock in the afternoon, Pliny's

69
00:03:38,823 --> 00:03:42,115
mother pointed out a cloud of unusual size and

70
00:03:42,115 --> 00:03:42,544
shape.

71
00:03:43,094 --> 00:03:46,065
His uncle called for his shoes and climbed to

72
00:03:46,065 --> 00:03:47,782
a spot with a better view.

73
00:03:48,331 --> 00:03:51,519
Pliny later compared it to an umbrella pine,

74
00:03:51,519 --> 00:03:54,634
a tall trunk rising high and then spreading

75
00:03:54,634 --> 00:03:55,938
out into branches.

76
00:03:56,489 --> 00:04:00,024
Today, eruptions like this one are called Plinian

77
00:04:00,024 --> 00:04:01,539
eruptions, after him.

78
00:04:02,090 --> 00:04:05,402
Volcanologists estimate the column rose more

79
00:04:05,402 --> 00:04:08,112
than thirty kilometers into the sky.

80
00:04:08,663 --> 00:04:12,295
Vesuvius has erupted many times since then.

81
00:04:12,846 --> 00:04:16,433
This film shows its most recent eruption, in

82
00:04:16,433 --> 00:04:20,183
1944, and that column was far smaller than the

83
00:04:20,183 --> 00:04:21,324
one Pliny saw.

84
00:04:21,875 --> 00:04:25,854
Inside the column of 79 was a mix of hot gas,

85
00:04:25,854 --> 00:04:27,269
ash, and pumice.

86
00:04:27,819 --> 00:04:31,375
Pumice is volcanic rock so full of gas bubbles

87
00:04:31,375 --> 00:04:33,463
that it can float on water.

88
00:04:34,012 --> 00:04:36,894
The wind was blowing toward the southeast.

89
00:04:37,444 --> 00:04:40,510
That put Pompeii, about ten kilometers from

90
00:04:40,510 --> 00:04:42,649
the vent, directly underneath.

91
00:04:43,199 --> 00:04:46,556
Within an hour or so, the sky over Pompeii went

92
00:04:46,556 --> 00:04:49,341
dark, and stones began to fall from it.

93
00:04:57,891 --> 00:05:00,322
The pumice came down like hail.

94
00:05:00,872 --> 00:05:03,727
It was not hot enough to burn, and each stone

95
00:05:03,727 --> 00:05:05,822
was light, but they did not stop.

96
00:05:06,372 --> 00:05:09,776
For about eighteen hours, pumice and ash rained

97
00:05:09,776 --> 00:05:12,892
down on Pompeii, piling up at about fifteen

98
00:05:12,892 --> 00:05:14,340
centimeters an hour.

99
00:05:14,891 --> 00:05:16,100
Streets filled.

100
00:05:16,649 --> 00:05:18,225
Doorways were blocked.

101
00:05:18,774 --> 00:05:22,122
By the end, the layer was nearly three meters

102
00:05:22,122 --> 00:05:22,494
deep.

103
00:05:23,043 --> 00:05:26,158
Many people fled, carrying what they could:

104
00:05:26,158 --> 00:05:29,345
coins, jewelry, keys, small statues of their

105
00:05:29,345 --> 00:05:30,432
household gods.

106
00:05:30,982 --> 00:05:34,003
Others sheltered indoors, hoping to wait it

107
00:05:34,003 --> 00:05:34,285
out.

108
00:05:34,834 --> 00:05:37,822
But the weight of the pumice on the roofs grew,

109
00:05:37,822 --> 00:05:39,857
and the roofs began to give way.

110
00:05:40,406 --> 00:05:43,250
Of the victims found at Pompeii, more than a

111
00:05:43,250 --> 00:05:46,029
third lay in the pumice layer, many of them

112
00:05:46,029 --> 00:05:48,290
crushed by falling roofs and walls.

113
00:05:48,841 --> 00:05:52,124
Across the bay, Pliny the Elder made a decision.

114
00:05:52,673 --> 00:05:55,748
A message arrived from a woman named Rectina,

115
00:05:55,748 --> 00:05:58,755
whose villa lay at the foot of the mountain,

116
00:05:58,755 --> 00:06:00,258
begging to be rescued.

117
00:06:00,809 --> 00:06:03,663
He ordered the warships of the fleet to launch.

118
00:06:04,213 --> 00:06:07,136
Others were fleeing the coast, but he steered

119
00:06:07,136 --> 00:06:09,929
straight toward the danger, dictating notes

120
00:06:09,929 --> 00:06:12,788
on the shape and movement of the cloud as he

121
00:06:12,788 --> 00:06:13,112
went.

122
00:06:13,663 --> 00:06:16,802
When his helmsman urged him to turn back, he

123
00:06:16,802 --> 00:06:19,869
is said to have replied: Fortune favors the

124
00:06:19,869 --> 00:06:20,298
brave.

125
00:06:20,848 --> 00:06:24,094
What had begun as a scientific trip became a

126
00:06:24,094 --> 00:06:25,201
rescue mission.

127
00:06:25,750 --> 00:06:27,757
His nephew stayed behind.

128
00:06:28,307 --> 00:06:30,803
He said he would rather keep studying.

129
00:06:31,353 --> 00:06:34,493
Near the shore, hot ash and pumice fell on the

130
00:06:34,493 --> 00:06:37,428
decks, and the coast was blocked by debris.

131
00:06:37,978 --> 00:06:41,344
So the admiral turned south, to Stabiae, where

132
00:06:41,344 --> 00:06:43,904
his friend Pomponianus had a villa.

133
00:06:44,454 --> 00:06:47,433
To calm everyone around him, he bathed, ate

134
00:06:47,433 --> 00:06:49,235
dinner, and went to sleep.

135
00:06:49,785 --> 00:06:52,455
During the night, the courtyard outside his

136
00:06:52,455 --> 00:06:55,560
room filled with ash and pumice, and the buildings

137
00:06:55,560 --> 00:06:55,932
shook.

138
00:06:59,483 --> 00:07:02,502
Then, sometime after midnight, the eruption

139
00:07:02,502 --> 00:07:03,064
changed.

140
00:07:03,613 --> 00:07:06,518
The great column began to collapse under its

141
00:07:06,518 --> 00:07:07,245
own weight.

142
00:07:07,795 --> 00:07:11,095
And when it fell, it became something far deadlier.

143
00:07:11,644 --> 00:07:15,365
A pyroclastic flow is an avalanche of superheated

144
00:07:15,365 --> 00:07:18,934
gas, ash, and rock that races across the ground

145
00:07:18,934 --> 00:07:22,123
at more than a hundred kilometers an hour.

146
00:07:22,673 --> 00:07:24,396
It cannot be outrun.

147
00:07:24,946 --> 00:07:28,459
The first one reached Herculaneum, on the coast

148
00:07:28,459 --> 00:07:29,954
west of the volcano.

149
00:07:30,504 --> 00:07:34,168
Flow after flow would bury the town under about

150
00:07:34,168 --> 00:07:37,754
twenty meters of volcanic material, far deeper

151
00:07:37,754 --> 00:07:38,767
than Pompeii.

152
00:07:39,317 --> 00:07:42,581
For centuries, archaeologists believed that

153
00:07:42,581 --> 00:07:45,846
almost everyone in Herculaneum had escaped.

154
00:07:49,396 --> 00:07:52,745
Then, in the nineteen eighties, excavators reached

155
00:07:52,745 --> 00:07:54,218
the ancient shoreline.

156
00:07:54,769 --> 00:07:57,947
In the stone boat sheds facing the sea, they

157
00:07:57,947 --> 00:07:59,103
found skeletons.

158
00:07:59,653 --> 00:08:01,120
Hundreds of them.

159
00:08:01,670 --> 00:08:04,705
More than three hundred people had gone down

160
00:08:04,705 --> 00:08:07,810
to the water, perhaps hoping to be rescued by

161
00:08:07,810 --> 00:08:08,155
boat.

162
00:08:08,704 --> 00:08:11,553
Most of the men were found on the beach, while

163
00:08:11,553 --> 00:08:14,216
women and children crowded into the vaults.

164
00:08:14,766 --> 00:08:17,550
The heat killed them almost instantly.

165
00:08:18,100 --> 00:08:21,289
And in a building in the town, a young man was

166
00:08:21,289 --> 00:08:23,230
found lying on a wooden bed.

167
00:08:23,781 --> 00:08:27,073
In 2020, researchers reported that fragments

168
00:08:27,073 --> 00:08:30,065
of his brain had been turned into glass.

169
00:08:38,615 --> 00:08:41,671
At dawn on the second day, there was no dawn.

170
00:08:42,221 --> 00:08:45,657
At Misenum, the young Pliny and his mother finally

171
00:08:45,657 --> 00:08:48,680
fled, and he described the darkness as being

172
00:08:48,680 --> 00:08:51,361
in a closed room with the lamp put out.

173
00:08:51,910 --> 00:08:55,141
He heard women shrieking, children crying, men

174
00:08:55,141 --> 00:08:55,774
shouting.

175
00:08:56,323 --> 00:08:59,101
People were calling for their parents, their

176
00:08:59,101 --> 00:09:01,941
children, their husbands and wives, trying to

177
00:09:01,941 --> 00:09:04,277
recognize each other by their voices.

178
00:09:04,826 --> 00:09:08,017
Many believed there were no gods left, and that

179
00:09:08,017 --> 00:09:11,139
this was the last, eternal night of the world.

180
00:09:11,690 --> 00:09:14,466
His mother begged him to leave her behind.

181
00:09:15,017 --> 00:09:17,971
She was old and slow, she said, and he could

182
00:09:17,971 --> 00:09:18,844
still escape.

183
00:09:19,394 --> 00:09:22,500
He refused, took her hand, and made her walk.

184
00:09:23,051 --> 00:09:25,767
Ash fell on them so thickly that they had to

185
00:09:25,767 --> 00:09:28,484
keep standing up and shaking it off, or they

186
00:09:28,484 --> 00:09:31,510
would have been buried and crushed by its weight.

187
00:09:35,059 --> 00:09:38,987
At Pompeii, around seven in the morning, a pyroclastic

188
00:09:38,987 --> 00:09:41,096
surge reached the city walls.

189
00:09:41,647 --> 00:09:43,441
It swept through the streets.

190
00:09:43,990 --> 00:09:47,041
No one who was still in the city survived.

191
00:09:47,591 --> 00:09:50,807
For a long time, it was thought that the victims

192
00:09:50,807 --> 00:09:52,548
had suffocated in the ash.

193
00:09:53,099 --> 00:09:56,470
But a study published in 2010 concluded that

194
00:09:56,470 --> 00:09:58,385
the main killer was heat.

195
00:09:58,936 --> 00:10:02,172
Even inside buildings, ten kilometers from the

196
00:10:02,172 --> 00:10:05,337
volcano, temperatures of at least two hundred

197
00:10:05,337 --> 00:10:08,363
and fifty degrees Celsius would have killed

198
00:10:08,363 --> 00:10:09,558
almost instantly.

199
00:10:10,109 --> 00:10:12,982
Their bodies were caught in the positions they

200
00:10:12,982 --> 00:10:14,419
were in at that moment.

201
00:10:14,970 --> 00:10:18,128
At Stabiae, the same morning, Pliny the Elder

202
00:10:18,128 --> 00:10:21,357
and his companions tied pillows to their heads

203
00:10:21,357 --> 00:10:24,515
to protect themselves from falling stones and

204
00:10:24,515 --> 00:10:26,129
went down to the shore.

205
00:10:26,679 --> 00:10:28,900
The sea was too rough to escape.

206
00:10:29,451 --> 00:10:32,479
Then came flames, and a smell of sulfur.

207
00:10:33,028 --> 00:10:35,811
The old admiral collapsed on the beach.

208
00:10:36,360 --> 00:10:40,051
His body was found two days later, intact, looking,

209
00:10:40,051 --> 00:10:43,308
his nephew wrote, more like a man asleep than

210
00:10:43,308 --> 00:10:43,669
dead.

211
00:10:44,220 --> 00:10:47,177
More flows followed, layer after layer.

212
00:10:47,726 --> 00:10:50,832
By the end of the second day, Pompeii lay under

213
00:10:50,832 --> 00:10:53,277
four to six meters of ash and pumice.

214
00:10:53,826 --> 00:10:57,312
When daylight finally returned to Misenum, the

215
00:10:57,312 --> 00:11:00,191
sun shone weakly, as if in an eclipse.

216
00:11:00,740 --> 00:11:04,033
Everything the young Pliny could see was buried

217
00:11:04,033 --> 00:11:06,134
in ash, as if under deep snow.

218
00:11:06,684 --> 00:11:09,855
Only the tops of a few walls showed above a

219
00:11:09,855 --> 00:11:11,330
new, grey landscape.

220
00:11:11,879 --> 00:11:15,432
And then, slowly, even the city's name was forgotten.

221
00:11:23,982 --> 00:11:26,719
For more than sixteen centuries, Pompeii lay

222
00:11:26,719 --> 00:11:28,399
under fields and vineyards.

223
00:11:28,948 --> 00:11:32,737
Digging at Herculaneum began in 1738, on the

224
00:11:32,737 --> 00:11:36,525
orders of the King of Naples, and at Pompeii

225
00:11:36,525 --> 00:11:37,903
ten years later.

226
00:11:38,452 --> 00:11:41,787
At first, the excavators did not even know which

227
00:11:41,787 --> 00:11:43,177
city they had found.

228
00:11:43,726 --> 00:11:47,534
It was only in 1763 that an inscription naming

229
00:11:47,534 --> 00:11:50,017
the town of Pompeii was found.

230
00:11:50,567 --> 00:11:54,003
The early excavations were treasure hunts: statues,

231
00:11:54,003 --> 00:11:57,102
paintings, and jewelry were carried off to the

232
00:11:57,102 --> 00:11:57,977
royal palace.

233
00:11:58,528 --> 00:12:02,411
That changed with an archaeologist named Giuseppe

234
00:12:02,411 --> 00:12:03,125
Fiorelli.

235
00:12:03,674 --> 00:12:08,486
Fiorelli took charge of the excavations in 1863.

236
00:12:09,037 --> 00:12:12,659
Workers had noticed strange hollows in the hardened

237
00:12:12,659 --> 00:12:14,649
ash, with bones inside them.

238
00:12:15,198 --> 00:12:18,010
Fiorelli understood what they were.

239
00:12:18,561 --> 00:12:21,618
The ash had hardened around the bodies of the

240
00:12:21,618 --> 00:12:24,675
victims, and as the bodies decayed, they left

241
00:12:24,675 --> 00:12:26,033
perfect empty molds.

242
00:12:26,583 --> 00:12:29,638
He had the hollows filled with liquid plaster.

243
00:12:30,187 --> 00:12:33,166
When the plaster set and the ash was chipped

244
00:12:33,166 --> 00:12:35,806
away, the people of Pompeii reappeared.

245
00:12:36,355 --> 00:12:37,870
Folds of clothing.

246
00:12:38,421 --> 00:12:39,663
Sandal straps.

247
00:12:40,212 --> 00:12:40,985
Hair.

248
00:12:41,535 --> 00:12:42,467
Faces.

249
00:12:43,018 --> 00:12:45,244
People lying where they fell.

250
00:12:45,793 --> 00:12:48,355
A dog twisting against its chain.

251
00:12:48,904 --> 00:12:52,752
More than a century later, in 2015, researchers

252
00:12:52,752 --> 00:12:56,191
began scanning the casts with CT machines.

253
00:12:56,740 --> 00:13:00,561
Inside, they found skeletons, and teeth in remarkably

254
00:13:00,561 --> 00:13:03,661
good condition, which they linked to a diet

255
00:13:03,661 --> 00:13:04,599
low in sugar.

256
00:13:05,149 --> 00:13:08,049
In a garden at the edge of the city, now called

257
00:13:08,049 --> 00:13:10,765
the Garden of the Fugitives, thirteen people

258
00:13:10,765 --> 00:13:12,000
were found together.

259
00:13:12,549 --> 00:13:16,087
The casts made Pompeii famous around the world.

260
00:13:16,638 --> 00:13:19,519
But they also made it easy to tell stories.

261
00:13:20,068 --> 00:13:22,970
Guides and scholars gave the figures roles:

262
00:13:22,970 --> 00:13:26,140
the mother, the lovers, the slave, the sisters.

263
00:13:26,691 --> 00:13:30,100
Those stories were based on posture, size, and

264
00:13:30,100 --> 00:13:30,692
jewelry.

265
00:13:31,243 --> 00:13:32,985
No one could test them.

266
00:13:33,534 --> 00:13:34,693
Until now.

267
00:13:43,244 --> 00:13:46,302
The first new evidence was about the date.

268
00:13:46,852 --> 00:13:49,838
For centuries, the eruption was dated to the

269
00:13:49,838 --> 00:13:52,891
twenty fourth of August, from the most common

270
00:13:52,891 --> 00:13:55,402
manuscript version of Pliny's letter.

271
00:13:55,951 --> 00:13:59,173
But the letter survives only in medieval copies,

272
00:13:59,173 --> 00:14:01,590
and the copies give different dates.

273
00:14:02,139 --> 00:14:05,169
And the archaeology never quite fit the summer.

274
00:14:05,720 --> 00:14:09,004
Victims seemed to wear heavy clothing, braziers

275
00:14:09,004 --> 00:14:12,289
had been in use, and the shops held autumn food

276
00:14:12,289 --> 00:14:13,548
like pomegranates.

277
00:14:14,097 --> 00:14:17,142
Wine jars had already been sealed after the

278
00:14:17,142 --> 00:14:18,134
grape harvest.

279
00:14:18,683 --> 00:14:21,970
A coin found with one victim carried a title

280
00:14:21,970 --> 00:14:25,256
of the emperor Titus that, according to many

281
00:14:25,256 --> 00:14:28,394
historians, he only received in September.

282
00:14:31,943 --> 00:14:35,578
Then, in 2018, archaeologists found a line of

283
00:14:35,578 --> 00:14:39,051
charcoal writing on a wall in a house being

284
00:14:39,051 --> 00:14:39,859
renovated.

285
00:14:40,408 --> 00:14:43,395
It gives a date: the sixteenth day before the

286
00:14:43,395 --> 00:14:46,382
Kalends of November, which is the seventeenth

287
00:14:46,382 --> 00:14:47,113
of October.

288
00:14:47,663 --> 00:14:50,604
It gives no year, but charcoal writing fades

289
00:14:50,604 --> 00:14:53,613
quickly, so it was probably written just days

290
00:14:53,613 --> 00:14:54,950
before the eruption.

291
00:14:55,500 --> 00:14:58,977
Many researchers now believe Vesuvius erupted

292
00:14:58,977 --> 00:15:02,222
on or around the twenty fourth of October.

293
00:15:05,773 --> 00:15:08,894
The second new evidence came from inside the

294
00:15:08,894 --> 00:15:10,100
casts themselves.

295
00:15:10,649 --> 00:15:13,585
Many of them still hold bones and teeth.

296
00:15:14,134 --> 00:15:17,959
In 2024, a team from the University of Florence,

297
00:15:17,959 --> 00:15:21,703
Harvard, and the Max Planck Institute published

298
00:15:21,703 --> 00:15:23,774
DNA from fourteen of them.

299
00:15:24,325 --> 00:15:27,460
In the House of the Golden Bracelet, an adult

300
00:15:27,460 --> 00:15:30,665
wearing a gold bracelet was found with a child

301
00:15:30,665 --> 00:15:31,571
on their lap.

302
00:15:32,120 --> 00:15:35,434
For generations, they were described as a mother

303
00:15:35,434 --> 00:15:36,400
and her child.

304
00:15:36,951 --> 00:15:40,083
The DNA showed that the adult was a man, and

305
00:15:40,083 --> 00:15:42,717
that he was not related to the child.

306
00:15:43,268 --> 00:15:46,322
Two other victims, locked in an embrace, had

307
00:15:46,322 --> 00:15:49,377
been imagined as sisters, or as a mother and

308
00:15:49,377 --> 00:15:50,001
daughter.

309
00:15:50,552 --> 00:15:53,601
At least one of them was genetically male.

310
00:15:54,152 --> 00:15:57,750
The DNA also showed that the people of Pompeii

311
00:15:57,750 --> 00:16:01,115
had diverse origins, many with recent roots

312
00:16:01,115 --> 00:16:03,384
in the eastern Mediterranean.

313
00:16:03,933 --> 00:16:06,752
The researchers did not say the old stories

314
00:16:06,752 --> 00:16:07,801
were impossible.

315
00:16:08,350 --> 00:16:11,502
They said those stories had been shaped by modern

316
00:16:11,502 --> 00:16:14,268
assumptions about family, about gender, and

317
00:16:14,268 --> 00:16:16,649
about who would hold whom at the end.

318
00:16:20,198 --> 00:16:23,494
The third new evidence came from Herculaneum,

319
00:16:23,494 --> 00:16:26,643
from a library of scrolls burned into lumps

320
00:16:26,643 --> 00:16:27,375
of carbon.

321
00:16:27,926 --> 00:16:31,346
Without opening one of them, researchers used

322
00:16:31,346 --> 00:16:34,691
CT scans and artificial intelligence to read

323
00:16:34,691 --> 00:16:37,048
fifteen columns of hidden text.

324
00:16:37,597 --> 00:16:40,955
The words had not been read for almost two thousand

325
00:16:40,955 --> 00:16:41,350
years.

326
00:16:49,900 --> 00:16:52,798
So, what really happened on the last day of

327
00:16:52,798 --> 00:16:53,336
Pompeii?

328
00:16:53,886 --> 00:16:57,498
Most people probably escaped: about eleven hundred

329
00:16:57,498 --> 00:17:00,676
and fifty bodies have been found in Pompeii,

330
00:17:00,676 --> 00:17:03,566
out of a town of around eleven thousand.

331
00:17:04,115 --> 00:17:07,215
Others may still lie in the unexcavated parts

332
00:17:07,215 --> 00:17:10,315
of the city, or on the roads where they fell.

333
00:17:10,865 --> 00:17:14,044
Those who died were killed by collapsing roofs

334
00:17:14,044 --> 00:17:17,086
in the night, or by the heat of the surge at

335
00:17:17,086 --> 00:17:17,432
dawn.

336
00:17:17,981 --> 00:17:20,933
They died, most likely, not in August but in

337
00:17:20,933 --> 00:17:24,421
the autumn, among the new wine and the pomegranates.

338
00:17:24,971 --> 00:17:27,543
And the people we have looked at for a century

339
00:17:27,543 --> 00:17:29,836
and a half were not quite who we thought.

340
00:17:30,386 --> 00:17:33,413
Not a mother, but a man holding a child who

341
00:17:33,413 --> 00:17:34,538
was not his own.

342
00:17:35,088 --> 00:17:38,751
Not sisters, but strangers, or friends, or something

343
00:17:38,751 --> 00:17:40,089
we will never know.

344
00:17:44,240 --> 00:17:47,976
Pompeii is often called a city frozen in time.

345
00:17:48,526 --> 00:17:52,057
But it is not frozen: every new tool, from the

346
00:17:52,057 --> 00:17:55,358
scanner to the DNA sequencer to the AI that

347
00:17:55,358 --> 00:17:58,659
reads burned scrolls, changes what it tells

348
00:17:58,659 --> 00:17:58,890
us.

349
00:17:59,440 --> 00:18:02,459
The city died in a single day, and we are still

350
00:18:02,459 --> 00:18:04,321
learning what happened in it.

351
00:18:11,872 --> 00:18:14,846
Thank you for watching Ancient World Files.
